        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "That's exactly right — and your reasoning is the right kind: the probability of a shape is just its share of the bag.\n\nWith 1 square and 3 round tiles, P(round) = 3/4 = 75% and P(square) = 1/4 = 25%. The order you dropped them in doesn't matter at all; only the counts do.\n\nNow let's see how that share behaves as the bag changes.\n\n```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=tile-draw-simulation\n\nroot = LearningSurface([sim], \"Move the bag's contents\", \"Change the mix of tiles and watch the chance of drawing a round one.\", \"ephemeral\")\nsim = Simulation(\"tile-draw\", \"Round or square?\", [\n  { id: \"rounds\", label: \"Round tiles in the bag\", unit: \"tiles\", min: 0, max: 12, step: 1, value: 3 },\n  { id: \"squares\", label: \"Square tiles in the bag\", unit: \"tiles\", min: 1, max: 12, step: 1, value: 1 }\n], [\n  { id: \"pRound\", label: \"Chance of drawing a round tile\", unit: \"%\", expr: \"rounds / (rounds + squares) * 100\", precision: 0, emphasis: true },\n  { id: \"pSquare\", label: \"Chance of drawing a square tile\", unit: \"%\", expr: \"squares / (rounds + squares) * 100\", precision: 0 },\n  { id: \"total\", label: \"Tiles in the bag\", unit: \"tiles\", expr: \"rounds + squares\", precision: 0 }\n], \"ephemeral\", \"First try a bag with 6 round tiles and 6 square tiles. Then try 9 rounds and 1 square. Notice what happens to the round chance — and check whether the square tile still has a chance to be drawn when rounds are 0.\")\n\n```"
          }
        ]
      },
